To scan a network for open (live) hosts when given a subnet (e.g., 172.18.50.0/24), use nmap's host discovery mode.
🔍 Basic Host Discovery (Ping Sweep)
nmap -sn 172.18.50.0/24
-sn (skip port scan): Only checks if hosts are up using ping, ARP, etc.

nmap -p 80,22 --open 172.18.50.0/24
--open: Show only IPs with those ports open

sudo nmap -O 172.18.50.0/24
May require root
Shows OS info of discovered hosts

nmap  -sn ->> search for which IPs/ host  are up 
-sC → Default Script Scan
-sC tells Nmap to run default NSE (Nmap Scripting Engine) scripts.
These scripts do things like:
Detect login banners (FTP, SSH, SMTP)
Pull SSL certificate info
Check for anonymous FTP
Test HTTP titles, headers, methods
Attempt version detection enhancements
It’s like basic recon automation using prewritten intelligence.
🔍 Example
nmap -sC 10.10.10.10
Returns:
SSH version
HTTP server banner
FTP anonymous login check
SSL cert details (if HTTPS)

✅ -sV → Service Version Detection
-sV tells Nmap to actively probe open ports to determine:
What service is running (e.g. Apache, nginx)
What version it is (e.g. Apache 2.4.38)
Nmap sends probes and reads responses to identify software — great for vulnerability matching

